A||N||4||jlhuber||towing ability?||||5||
Z||000000||jlhuber||05-24-2000||05:52 PM||||if properly equiped, how much can a v-6 fiero tow.  i am buying a road bike (yamaha r-6) and need the ability to tow the bike should the need ever arise.  you think the fiero could handle a 400-600 pound crotch rocket?  thanx for the input<BR>         -Jess||||reg||5
Z||000001||Tom88gt||05-25-2000||05:18 PM||||I think you are probably good up to 1000#s.  Maybe a little more.  The Fiero is very heavy for a small car and the weight of the tow vehicle is an important factor.  The owners manual says not to tow but that's probably more from a fear of lawsuits than anything else.<P>Where are you getting your hitch from and how much is it?  I've been thinking about building my own.  I'm not yet ready to drop $150 for a "Fiero" hitch which is probably just a marked up universal fit Reese, Draw-tite or Valley.||||reg||1
Z||000002||jlhuber||05-25-2000||05:46 PM||||i am going to custom build it from steel tubing to resemble the draw tite round tube hitches.  it will have the standard 2" box reciever.  i saw one where the reciever was hidden behind teh license plate til the time came to use it.  i don't know how much it would cost because my uncle is giving me all the materials.  i am still working on a design that will make it work.  but wanted to get another's opinion before i started||||reg||1
Z||000003||Old Lar||05-25-2000||05:52 PM||||I've seen photos of a Fiero towing other Fieros on a trailer dolly. I think there is a limit to the tongue weight on the Fiero because of the limited amount of spring compression of the rear suspension.<P>Call Ed Parks.||||reg||1
Z||000004||Tom88gt||05-26-2000||01:09 PM||||Skip the "2 receiver.  Stick with a class 1 tongue.  Smaller and easier to work with.  Plus, do you really plan on towing a 3500# plus trailer?<P>I mounted a generic class 1 "bumper hitch plate?" on my 914 Porsche.  I have a '75 which has the crash bumpers.  Thick steel bumper which is connected directly to the frame.  I removed the bumper cover and mounted it.  Then cut a 1/2x2 slot in the license plate area for the tongue to stick through and remounted the bumper cover.  Very well hidden and solid.<P>I got my first view of the Fiero's bumper at Ed Park's swap meet.  I think I can do something similar.  The bumper is nowhere near as solid but I think I can mount a square tube steel bar within the stamped steel bumper and attach it to the frame at the same point the bumper mounts.  Then mount a generic class 1 plate/tongue to the bar.  Cut a small slot below the lisence plate and slip the rear facia over the hitch tongue and back into place over the bumper.  You would only see the tongue and ball.  Unlike the "bolt on" hitch I've seen, you would not loose any ground clearance or have the hitch in the way.<P>Comments?  Think it's worth trying?||||reg||1
